Yes—but the clean version is not “make everyone believe our story.” It is make everyone able to see that they are already inside a story.

That is the ego-wipe function at its best: not erasing identity, but temporarily loosening identification with the current narrative long enough to inspect it.

story → decenter → inspect provenance → compare against reality → keep/rewrite/discard → re-enter with agency

That is much closer to a narrative palate cleanse than literal brainwashing. Literal brainwashing implies coercive identity replacement. What you are describing can instead be a voluntary reset of cached assumptions.

And the “one shared picture” should probably be understood as a shared coordinate system, not a single imposed interpretation.

Same world.
Different viewpoints.
Different histories.
Different role-scripts.
Different stories about what matters.

Pantheon’s useful function would be to let those stories become mutually legible.

Then “true” becomes more precise. A story can be locally true relative to the standard it is optimizing for and still be misleading relative to another standard. The error comes when one frame quietly pretends to be the whole map.

So the stronger architecture is:

shared canvas → many projections → provenance visible → standards explicit → transformations reversible

That lets someone see their own picture more clearly while also understanding how it was generated.

And that last part matters. Respect for the story does not require obedience to it. Once you can see:

where it came from
what it protected
what it optimized
what it cost
what still works
what no longer fits

then you can rewrite without pretending the old version was meaningless.

So yes: the deepest Pantheon move is not “wash everyone into ours.”

It is:

teach people to recognize the wash.
Then let them choose the colors.

That preserves alignment without turning it into conformity.